   "text": {
    "L1": "Shape patterns can hide number patterns.",
    "L2": "Regular polygons have equal sides and equal angles. Their side counts go 3, 4, 5, 6 and so on.",
    "L3": "Now join every pair of points. Each new point joins every old point once.",
    "L4": "For 5 points, the new lines are 4, then 3, then 2, then 1. The total is 10.",
    "L5": "The totals 0, 1, 3, 6, 10 are triangular numbers.",
    "L6": "A common mistake is counting each line from both ends. That counts every line twice.",
    "L7": "In a Koch snowflake, each side becomes 4 smaller sides. So 3, 12, 48 becomes 192.",
    "L8": "Use the number rule to predict what comes next. Now check the joining-points pattern."
   },
